Revision history for skilletor and Skilletor, its Perl distribution

{{$NEXT}}
  - The Perl distribution Skilletor moves from perl/ to the repository root
    and is released with dzil; its version 0.008 is the plugin's 0.8.0, which
    the Perl CLI now reports. Standalone binaries for linux-x86_64,
    linux-arm64, macos-arm64 and windows-x86_64 come from the house
    dzil-binary workflow, with OpenSSL linked statically; the smoke proves an
    https url source. skilletor is licensed under the Artistic License 2.0
    from this release (k242).
  - The plugin's hooks and bin/skilletor run bin/skilletor-start: a cached
    release binary verified against checksums pinned in the plugin, else
    Node.js >= 18, else Perl >= 5.40 from source. Without pinned checksums
    nothing is downloaded. Windows stays on Node through bin/skilletor.exe.
    The Hermes adapter takes a checkout's bin/skilletor without dist/ on
    POSIX (k191).
  - Conformance path comparison permits exactly one additional JSON-
    stringification layer only within complete paired escaped-quote intervals
    in one complete outer scalar. Wrong depths, unbalanced quotes, payload or
    formatting changes and unrelated bytes still fail (k237).
  - Published-layout catalog entries use platform-independent UTF-8 byte
    ordering, matching Linux and Perl instead of native directory order or
    JavaScript UTF-16 order (k240).
  - Conformance verification uses expected-oracle path-token provenance and
    encoding-aware separator matching. Raw streams and files retain byte order
    and exact formatting; engine-result JSON arrays remain deliberately
    unordered while preserving duplicate multiplicity. Windows verification
    still runs; only expectation-update authoring is refused on Windows before
    writes and points authors to POSIX (k237).
  - Cross-platform fixtures set exact Git-index modes per commit, seed the
    prior Hermes home explicitly, use portable decoded transport paths, retry
    transient cleanup failures within a bound, and make CRLF conversion
    idempotent (k237, k239).
  - On Windows, existing absolute project paths use native canonical spelling
    so 8.3 and long aliases share scope, pending-report and last-check identity;
    POSIX keys retain their stored spelling (k238).
  - Managed `.gitignore` and `.worktreeinclude` edits recognize CRLF, leave
    unchanged blocks byte-identical, and preserve every byte outside their
    markers, including EOF separators, during update or removal (k239).
  - Conformance and platform fixtures isolate Windows home and temporary roots,
    compare escaped platform paths, form Git file URLs portably, constrain
    runtime-added environment values, and remove checkout-spelling assumptions
    from build, Git and Hermes tests (k237).
  - The ordinary CI matrix now includes a Windows Node 24 leg with typecheck,
    tests and dist verification. Native-only launcher probes cover version and
    hook startup from isolated paths containing spaces and non-ASCII
    characters (k237).
  - An included template's syntax error is now a synchronous item warning
    rather than a delayed crash; unaffected items still install (k160).
  - Skill install paths no longer consult the process cwd. Commands that need
    a project context can run from a removed cwd when given `--project-dir`;
    without one they exit 1 with an actionable diagnostic instead of a stack
    trace (k192, k193).
  - SessionStart now gives source checking, lock waiting and source resolution
    a 20-second pre-apply budget. The per-scope guard names the late scope
    without implying that earlier scope writes were rolled back (k151).
  - Frontmatter parsing, Codex agent conversion and hook state preserve
    prototype-named keys as own data without inherited collisions or prototype
    mutation (k194).
  - `install.sh` has a fail-safe CLI-only path for machines without Node. It
    preflights a candidate checkout's verified binary or source-Perl starter,
    leaves Claude plugin metadata untouched, and preserves the working command
    and checkout on failure (k230).
  - The prepared starter keeps per-prompt hooks offline, backs off failed
    session-start provisioning for 15 minutes, and rechecks cached binaries
    before execution. It remains unwired from the default plugin hooks (k231).
  - The source Perl port now requires Perl 5.40. Perl CI tests that version and
    reproducibly provisions its pinned Text::Nunjucks dependency (k159).
  - The prepared binary workflow resolves its template dependency repository
    and revision from the selected skilletor source revision. Its isolated
    smoke covers every ported command without widening platform or activation
    claims (k219, k232).
  - Project scopes now select explicit project targets or genuine harness
    evidence in that project instead of every machine target. Skilletor-owned
    artifacts do not count as evidence; an ambiguous multi-harness project is
    preserved and warned, and a sole machine target remains the bootstrap
    fallback. TypeScript and Perl share cases 141-149 (k229).
  - Automatic Claude plugin requirements are skipped, without check drift,
    when their virtual settings would change inferred project targets. Items
    still sync, and explicit project targets permit the settings update
    (k234).
  - The Perl CLI implements install and uninstall: wildcards, bundles,
    prompts, missing-source and refusal diagnostics, and scope-aware removal.
    Shared cases 130-139 and 200-205 hold both CLIs to the same behavior
    (k178, k179).
  - Both engines reject lock entries that are not objects and invalid
    string-valued ownership maps before writing. `available` compares item
    names without case while preserving source-alias case; cases 151-153 pin
    the behavior (k222, k236).
  - Plain items no longer ask the operating system for a template user. Both
    engines create render context lazily, once, only when a `.njk` file needs
    it (k161).
  - Hook JSON that is null or another non-object, or has a non-string `cwd`,
    fails open without an internal TypeError. The Perl conformance transport
    preserves explicit JSON null; case 140 pins both CLIs (k200, k228).
  - The Perl port decodes non-ASCII harness paths from the environment, emits
    its ambiguous-source en dash as Unicode, and joins frontmatter UTF-16
    surrogate pairs like the reference. Cases 126-127 pin the paths (k163,
    k195, k198).
  - Real Hermes child tests exclude inherited credentials, and Perl report
    conformance follows intervening CLI steps before comparing later engine
    results (k154, k197).
  - Documentation reflects manifest version ownership, conditional Claude
    settings updates, and the Perl port's current Claude, Codex and Hermes,
    template and CLI support without claiming binary activation (k157, k226,
    k227, k233).
  - Builds support explicitly linked local dependencies in worktrees, while
    refusing tools or bundled inputs resolved from another checkout (k148,
    k220).
  - Conformance cases 93-97: the CLI's available – listing and installed marks, items that cannot be built, bundles, escaping, refusals (k177).
  - Conformance cases 110-125: the Hermes target in sync, check and status, its home move and upgrade, and the Hermes hooks (k185).
  - Conformance cases 83-92: the CLI's add, its refusals and the probe of a generic host (k176).
  - Conformance cases 100-108: the Codex target in sync, check and status, and the Codex hooks (k184).
  - Conformance cases 72-82: the CLI's trust, source list and source remove (k175).
  - A release-binaries workflow, prepared and not active (workflow_dispatch
    only): it packs the Perl port with PAR::Packer for linux-x86_64,
    linux-arm64, macos-arm64 and windows-x86_64, smokes each binary on clean
    machines without Perl, and on a tag run attaches the binaries, tarballs and
    one checksums file to the GitHub release vX.Y.Z, with the checksums for
    bin/skilletor-binaries.sha256 as an artifact. A run without a tag is a
    probe that touches no release. Its Windows leg packs Win32::Process and
    runs the full smoke, the hooks included (k210).
  - bin/skilletor-start, a plugin starter prepared for the Perl binaries and
    not wired yet (hooks and the Hermes adapter still run bin/skilletor): it
    runs the release binary cached under <claudeConfigDir>/skilletor/bin/,
    else downloads it once and runs it only if its checksum matches the one
    pinned in the plugin, else Node >= 18, else Perl >= 5.40; a `hook` call
    exits 0 on every path. Without pinned checksums it downloads nothing.
  - A conformance step `cli` runs the CLI itself (`skilletor sync|check|status`
    with its arguments, exit code and stdout/stderr compared), and the case
    knob `gitInit` (`["project"]`, `["home"]`) makes those directories git
    checkouts; the Perl port's CLI is held to the reference this way. Cases 41
    to 45 cover the project root, an unused and a dropped project, the status
    target naming and `--force`.
  - Conformance cases 65 to 71 hold both CLIs to the same bytes on the context paths: no agent harness detected (new case knob `"claude": false`, no Claude Code marker written), the project dir being the home, a lock entry an interrupted sync left partial, a rule that renders empty, an agent's missing briefing skills, and a source's name or address with control and bidi characters escaped on every stream (k87).
  - A sync resolves its sources in parallel, but now reports what became of
    each - a failure, a fall back to the cache, a trust request - in the order
    the sources are declared, the order the Perl port's sequential resolve
    gives. Two such sources no longer swap places in the report text and
    --json from run to run.
  - The Claude Code plugin takes its version from the commit, so every push to
    main reaches marketplace users with auto-update.
  - `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` is Claude Code's config dir and now the user scope's
    `.claude` everywhere: the user config, the lock, the installed items, the
    state dir, the briefing lookup, the plugin state read for the project's
    `.claude/settings.json` requirement and the Claude detection markers
    (`<dir>/.claude.json`, `settings.json`, `projects`). Default
    `<home>/.claude`; an empty value counts as unset. The Perl port does the
    same; conformance cases 31 and 32 pin it for both engines.
  - Three inputs are refused instead of read loosely. A source's
    `skilletor.json` with a `vars` that is no object (an array was spread by
    index) fails the source's scan. A hand-edited lock whose `via` is no array
    of strings (a string matched bundle labels by substring) is an invalid
    lock. A url tarball cut short, inside an entry or without its
    end-of-archive block, is a corrupt archive (the cache stays) instead of a
    smaller one. The Perl port does the same; conformance case 33 pins the
    vars.
  - Five oddities the Perl port found are fixed. A source serving two foreign
    entries of one bundle asks for trust once, not twice. A `\U` escape above
    U+10FFFF in a bundle file is that bundle's warning instead of an error
    that aborted the whole source scan. A `SKILL.md`, agent or rule with CRLF
    line ends or a BOM keeps its description in the catalog. An install key
    only `Object.prototype` knows (`constructor`, `__proto__`) is an unknown
    type, and a spec such as `constructor/foo` is no forge link.
    `uninstall` no longer throws on a non-string entry in an install list.
    Conformance cases 13 to 16 pin them for both engines.
  - A `url` source serving a tarball with a corrupt header size no longer
    hangs a sync: the update fails as a corrupt archive and keeps the last
    good cache, with a warning.
  - `conformance/` holds cases (config, source trees, the expected
    resolution, and for sync cases the files, lock, check and status) that
    the TypeScript engine and the Perl port both run;
    `SKILLETOR_CONFORMANCE_UPDATE=1` regenerates them from the TypeScript
    reference. Cases cover removal, conflicts and `--force`, check drift,
    `"gitignore": false`, briefing, settings.json and executable bits;
    fixture names starting with `.git` are stored as `dot.git...` so a
    checkout holds every fixture.
  - `install.sh` sets skilletor up from a plain shell (GitHub issue #1):
    `curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Getty/skilletor/main/install.sh | sh`
    installs the plugin when `claude` is on `PATH` (or updates it) and writes
    a `~/.local/bin/skilletor` that survives plugin updates; without Claude
    Code, or with `--no-plugin`, it clones the repository and links
    `bin/skilletor`. `--bin-dir` picks the directory. The README has it under
    "From the command line"; the Hermes docs name the command `SKILLETOR_BIN`.
  - A session that moves into another checkout of a skilletor project (a
    plain `git worktree add`, say) gets that checkout's project scope synced,
    so the worktree completes itself: one line names the directory, and the
    model is told what landed. In Claude Code a Bash `cd` is awaited (a
    `PostToolUse` hook), so the next tool call finds the items; other moves
    are seen by `CwdChanged`, which is not awaited. A Codex or Hermes session
    moves only between turns, and the next prompt waits for the sync; the
    Codex `UserPromptSubmit` timeout is 30 s, the Hermes plugin's per-turn
    limit 25 s. Under Hermes, when skills landed, the line names
    `hermes skills trust <dir>`; skilletor never reads or writes Hermes trust.
    After updating, trust the changed hooks again in Codex `/hooks` and run
    `hermes plugins update skilletor` for the Hermes plugin. The Perl port has
    no hooks.
  - A hook run from a working directory that no longer exists, or whose own
    setup failed, exited 1 with a stack trace; it now exits 0 with at most one
    warning line, and no longer runs `git rev-parse` on the process cwd when
    its project is known. `hook ... --scope bogus` exits 0 too: the hook is
    dispatched before the option checks.
  - The Perl CLI loads each module by the first call that needs it:
    --version, --help and a usage error in ASCII load Skilletor::CLI alone
    (180 -> 19 ms with perl -Ilib), and the prompt hook with nothing due no
    longer loads Skilletor::Report and what it stands on. Output and exit
    codes are unchanged. t/68-cli.t pins what the argument layer loads,
    t/69-hooks.t the modules of the prompt hook's fast path (k199).
  - Skilletor::Frontmatter::Float and Skilletor::TOML::Float are hidden from
    the PAUSE indexer: inner value classes without a .pm of their own, no
    longer listed in META's provides (k202).
  - `skilletor available [source] [--json]` in the Perl CLI
    (Skilletor::Commands::available, after cmdAvailable): every trusted
    source - or the one named - resolved and scanned under the sync lock, the
    caches swept first; an item marked installed when either scope declares it
    or either lock holds it for that source (any target, not a skipped entry),
    a bundle when either config declares it; an item's error, a bundle's
    sorted members, `(no items)` or its expansion error on a second line;
    --json the list; an untrusted source skipped. A lock entry that is no
    object marks nothing installed, where the reference throws a TypeError
    (k222). Skilletor::Error::Command's POD names add's and available's
    refusals. Conformance cases 93 to 97, t/76-commands-available.t (k177).
  - The Perl engine now syncs, checks and reports the Hermes target as the
    reference does (spec section 16): skills only, in the Hermes home's
    skills ($HERMES_HOME, else the recorded home, else ~/.hermes) and
    <project>/.hermes/skills, with no managed .gitignore block. It is never
    detected, a target only through the user config's "targets"; in a project
    with Codex active, the Codex copy serves Hermes. One home per user-scope
    lock, recorded in hermes-home.json: a sync under another home removes the
    old home's items with a warning and installs anew, and check counts it as
    targetsChanged. Once hermes is no target, a sync removes its entries from
    the recorded home and drops the record. Skilletor::Engine has a new
    hermes_home attribute and a hermes_opt_in_hint method. Under --harness
    hermes, SessionStart ends with the opt-in line while hermes is no target,
    and the prompt hook follows a move, naming `hermes skills trust <dir>`
    once a skill Hermes loads landed (k152). Tested in t/74-engine-hermes.t
    and t/75-hooks-hermes.t (k185).
  - A Skilletor::Error stringifies to its message, as its documentation says:
    an error no one catches prints the message, not
    Skilletor::Error::Config=HASH(0x...). namespace::clean removed the subs
    that hold Error.pm's overload table; the overload is now declared after
    it. The CLI and the hooks print what they did before - they ask an error
    for its message (k213).
  - `skilletor add [name] <spec> [--project]` in the Perl CLI
    (Skilletor::Commands::add, after cmdAdd): the same checks in the same
    order - the k95 name, no project scope, the spec, the k110 definition, the
    k101 taken name, the k119 ref, the k126 path, the k116 kept entry. It then
    trusts the source and syncs, prints the added or kept line, and prints the
    k103 saved lines when the sync fails (k176).
  - New Skilletor::Probe: `git ls-remote -- url` (5 s), then an in-process
    HEAD on url.tar.gz through HTTP::Tiny with fetch's headers, so a packed
    binary needs neither Node nor Perl for it. Tested in t/31-probe.t (k176).
  - The Codex target in the Perl engine: sync, check and status install
    skills in .agents/skills, agents as TOML and rules as sections of one
    skilletor-rules.md with the pointer block in the Codex AGENTS.md - its
    warnings (malformed markers, a link, a directory or Claude Code's
    CLAUDE.md in its place, AGENTS.override.md, project_doc_max_bytes) and the
    migration of a first-design rules block as the reference has them - and
    warn while Codex has not trusted skilletor's SessionStart hook
    (Skilletor::AgentsMD, after agentsmd.ts; Skilletor::Error::Block).
    Hermes followed in k185. The hooks under --harness codex: a new
    session's context starts with the rules files, and the prompt hook syncs
    the checkout a session moved into, its answer joined with a pending
    report (k184).
  - `skilletor trust`, `skilletor source list [--json]` and `skilletor source
    remove <name> [--project] [--force]` in the Perl CLI (Skilletor::Commands,
    after the reference's commands.ts): the same checks in the same order (an
    undeclared name and where else it is declared, an entry other files'
    overrides still need, the one entry config load refuses removed, installed
    items without --force), the same messages and exit codes, the config
    rewritten as two-space JSON in its key order. Skilletor::Config gains
    has_source_entry (an entry written as null is declared). New conformance
    cases 72 to 82 hold both CLIs to the same bytes.
  - t/54-parity-cli.t runs the reference CLI (node dist/skilletor.js) and
    bin/skilletor on one machine, in the same paths, and holds the port to the
    same bytes: exit codes, both streams, installed trees, the lock and the
    state files (render-inputs, sources-read, unreached, the hooks' marks and
    records), over five conformance cases, git's failure wording, the real
    host and user in templates, and one sync lock that either CLI holds while
    the other's sync and SessionStart wait or time out. It skips without node
    or the bundle.
  - Skilletor::Process runs programs on Windows: stdin, stdout and stderr as
    temp files on the standard handles of a P_NOWAIT spawn, the program found
    on PATH as Node finds it (never in the cwd first, never a .bat or .cmd),
    its arguments quoted for the C runtime, a timeout terminating it
    (reported as signal 15, as Node does there); spawn_detached starts the
    hooks' background through Win32::Process (new Windows-only dependency)
    with no inherited handle and a console without a window.
    Skilletor::State refreshes the sync lock on Windows while it waits on a
    program, and stamps times with the core utime there (Time::HiRes has
    none). Untested on real Windows (exercised on Linux through an injected
    seam).
  - `skilletor hook cwd-changed|post-tool-use` for Claude Code: a session
    that moves into another checkout gets that checkout's project scope
    synced – its git top level, never CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR – when its lock no
    longer matches its config (a local check, no source asked), with a 20 s
    deadline past which nothing is written and a wait on the sync lock up to
    it; one line names the checkout and what to run there, PostToolUse adds
    a context for the model. New conformance cases 60 to 64 hold both CLIs
    to the same bytes.
  - `skilletor hook session-start|user-prompt-submit` for Claude Code: the
    SessionStart check and sync, the prompt's interval check with a detached
    background sync (a packed binary re-runs itself via PAR_PROGNAME), and
    pending reports. Every failure is one warning line with exit 0, and a
    prompt with nothing due never loads the engine. New conformance cases 50
    to 59 hold both CLIs to the same hook output.
  - Renders Nunjucks templates as the reference does, through Text::Nunjucks
    (a Perl port of Nunjucks 3.2.4, not on CPAN yet; new dependency, Perl
    5.40): a sync installs .njk items rendered with the reference's context,
    skips an item whose main template renders empty and words template errors
    the same. resolve_skill and its siblings still refuse a templated item.
  - The sync, check and status reports keep the reference's key order, and
    Skilletor::Report's new report_json writes them byte for byte as
    skilletor sync|check|status --json prints them.
  - A command line, bin/skilletor over Skilletor::CLI: the reference's usage
    text, version (the plugin's, provisional), option checks, usage errors and
    exit codes, byte for byte; a command not ported yet says so and exits 1.
    Skilletor::Process::run_command feeds stdin through a
    pipe when given one.
  - The engine options the hooks set, as the reference has them: check's local
    (no source asked), the sync's if_changed (a local check under the sync
    lock), deadline and a per-call lock_timeout_ms. Skilletor::State keeps
    pending-report.json and session-cwd.json (the 256 sessions recorded last)
    byte for byte as the reference does, and gains due_again;
    Skilletor::Report's new report_hook words a sync report as the hooks do.
    report_text gives an item with an unknown harness prefix its type's
    activation hint.
  - Skilletor::Convert and Skilletor::TOML port the reference's Codex
    conversion: an agent as a Codex agent-role TOML file (the codex:
    passthrough, its warnings, the # briefing: skills line), a rule as its
    section of the Codex rules file, and the per-target mapping of an item's
    output, byte for byte; not yet used by a sync.
  - The CLI runs sync, check and status as the reference does: the report
    text and --json, check's exit 0, 1 or 2, status with the target naming,
    --scope, --force and --project-dir; the project root is the git top level
    of the working directory (2 s, else the directory itself). The
    conformance runner's cli steps hold it to the reference's bytes.
  - scripts/ builds bin/skilletor into one standalone executable with
    PAR::Packer (build-binary.sh), proves it byte for byte against perl -Ilib
    (verify-binary.sh), checks its shared libraries and glibc floor
    (check-binary-libs.sh), and runs the shared conformance cases' cli steps
    against it in a container with neither perl nor node (container-proof.sh);
    scripts/ stays out of the CPAN tarball.
  - scripts/smoke-binary.sh runs a packed binary through a sync, check and
    status of the t/data/smoke fixture on a clean machine without Perl
    (t/70-smoke-binary.t), and the Claude Code hooks, the detached
    background sync included (k205); verify-binary.sh now also works with
    git before 2.32. scripts/loaded-modules.pl packs Win32::Process on
    MSWin32 and runs on Windows, and the release-binaries Windows leg runs the
    full smoke (k210).

0.001     2026-10-24 03:08:52Z
  - Follows the reference's k145: CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR is the user scope's
    .claude (config, lock, roots, state dir, markers, briefing lookup); a
    source's non-object vars, a lock via that is no array of strings and a
    truncated url tarball are errors.
  - Initial release: the Perl port of skilletor, beside the TypeScript
    reference in the same repository, sharing its config files, its
    ~/.claude/skilletor/cache and its trust.json.
  - Skilletor::Config loads the user, project and local config with the
    reference's merge and precedence rules (per-field source merge with
    origins, author mode, entries without a backend, "ref": null, vars,
    gitignore, claudeSettings, targets), reports the same errors in the same
    order, and edits a config file as `add`/`install`/`uninstall` do.
  - Skilletor::Entry parses [type:]name@source, patterns and
    [bundle:]name@source; Skilletor::Spec resolves `add` shorthands (forge
    links, scp-like and transport::address forms, probed generic hosts).
  - Sources: Skilletor::Source::Local, ::Git (shallow fetch, refs, SHA pins,
    offline cache reuse, repair and restore after killed runs, lock-file
    sweep) and ::Url (https tarball, ETag or sha256 version, staged swap,
    sweep). Skilletor::Cache names cache directories as the reference does,
    so both implementations share one fetch.
  - Skilletor::Backend picks a source's backend (author mode) for trust and
    fetch alike; Skilletor::State reads and writes trust.json;
    Skilletor::Targets detects harnesses and merges targets.
  - Skilletor::JSON parses like JSON.parse (key order, V8's messages) and
    writes like JSON.stringify(value, null, 2), byte for byte;
    Skilletor::URL, ::Gzip, ::Tar, ::JSString and ::FSUtil carry the Node
    behaviour that reaches a name, a byte or a message.
  - Skilletor resolves skills, agents and rules as a sync would install
    them, without installing: resolve_skill, resolve_agent, resolve_rule
    and resolve_rules give the main file in the source tree or the shared
    cache, resolve_item, resolve_items and items the whole record. Both
    scopes declaring a name follow Claude Code's precedence: the personal
    skill wins, the project's agent wins, rules of both scopes apply (the
    user's first) – resolve_rule throws Skilletor::Error::Ambiguous rather
    than hide one. fetch => always, missing or never
    picks the network use; no agent harness is needed (require_harness
    restores the reference's check). An item with a .njk file is flagged
    templated and refused as an include (Skilletor::Error::Unsupported):
    the port renders no templates.
  - Skilletor::Resolver ports the reference's resolveItems: trust, sources
    into the cache, catalogs, wildcards, bundles with their vars and
    entries of other sources, the overlap rules and the lock's kept
    offers, with the reference's warnings and report, under its sync lock.
  - Skilletor::Catalog scans a source tree (layout, plugin.json, the
    Claude project layout, a source's path, item errors),
    Skilletor::Bundles parses and expands bundle files, and
    Skilletor::Frontmatter reads the reference's YAML subset, with its
    error messages. Skilletor::Lock reads lock files.
  - Skilletor::State takes the sync lock (sync.lock/) as the reference
    does, so the two exclude each other; the git, url and local sources
    gain cached, their cache without the network.
  - Skilletor::Tar refuses a header whose size field is no non-negative
    number as a corrupt archive ("corrupt tar entry (size): <name>"), as
    the reference now does: a url source falls back to its cache.
  - Skilletor->sync, ->check and ->status (Skilletor::Engine) port the
    reference's commands for the Claude Code target: the same installed
    files and executable bits, lock, .gitignore blocks, .worktreeinclude
    and .claude/settings.json plugin requirement (k140), conflicts and
    force, removal of what is no longer declared, partial entries after a
    failed write, the briefing check, the same JSON reports and the same
    state records (render-inputs.json, sources-read.json, unreached.json),
    so either implementation's check accepts what the other synced. A
    scope targeting Codex or Hermes, or a sync where the CLI left their
    state, is refused with nothing touched. A .njk item is never installed
    unrendered: the sync warns, keeps what the CLI installed, and leaves the
    scope open for the CLI's next sync.
  - Skilletor::Apply, ::Render (copy only), ::Gitignore, ::ClaudeSettings,
    ::Briefing and ::Report port apply.ts, the copy half of render.ts,
    gitignore.ts, claudesettings.ts, briefing.ts and report.ts (with the
    CLI's status text and exit codes); Skilletor::Lock writes locks,
    Skilletor::State keeps the records check reads, Skilletor::Targets has
    the layout table, Skilletor::JSON takes a string indent.
  - t/60-conformance.t runs every step of the shared conformance cases;
    t/52-parity-sync.t has the reference and the port sync one machine and
    compares every byte they leave.
  - Tests replay oracles recorded from the reference (t/data) and, in a
    checkout with node, drive the reference and the port against the same
    remotes and cache (t/50-parity.t).

0.7.0     2026-10-16 20:00:22Z
  - The plugin, package and README descriptions name Hermes Agent as the third
    target.
  - The Hermes plugin installs with Hermes' own installer:
    `hermes plugins install Getty/skilletor/hermes/skilletor --enable`, then
    `hermes plugins update skilletor`. It does not bring the CLI: name a clone's
    `bin/skilletor` as `SKILLETOR_BIN` in `$HERMES_HOME/.env`, or put
    `skilletor` on `PATH`; Hermes shows how after the install. The hand-linked
    install still runs the checkout's CLI. The bundled skill states this setup
    and that `["hermes"]` alone in `targets` drops Claude and Codex.
  - `bin/skilletor` follows its own symlinks to find `dist/`, so a link on
    `PATH` (`ln -s <clone>/bin/skilletor ~/.local/bin/skilletor`), `npm link`
    and `npm install -g` run the CLI instead of failing with "Cannot find
    module".
  - The bundled skill is shorter, with source, Hermes and sync detail in its
    references.
  - A project sync makes the plugin a requirement of the project: in the
    committed `.claude/settings.json` it enables `skilletor@<marketplace>`,
    the marketplace Claude Code installed skilletor from, and declares in
    `extraKnownMarketplaces` every marketplace an `enabledPlugins` entry there
    needs (`briefing@getty` too), so a fresh checkout offers to install them.
    The report says what it added and asks to commit the file once. It only
    adds: existing keys and the file's formatting stay, `false` entries are
    respected, a marketplace with a source on this machine's disk is never
    written, and a file that does not parse is left alone with a warning. It
    runs for projects with a committed `skilletor.json` and Claude Code as a
    target; `"claudeSettings": false` turns it off. A config with
    `claudeSettings` is a config error for skilletor 0.6.0 and earlier. The
    README keeps the snippet for the standalone CLI under "Sharing a project:
    require the plugin". Codex has no equivalent.

0.6.0     2026-10-15 17:39:19Z
  - Hermes Agent is a third target, `hermes` in `targets`. It is opt-in:
    never detected, so `"targets"` in `~/.claude/skilletor.json` must name it,
    together with every other harness in use (`["claude", "hermes"]`). Skills
    install into the Hermes home (`$HERMES_HOME`, else Hermes' own default:
    `~/.hermes`, `%LOCALAPPDATA%\hermes` on Windows) and, in a project, into
    `.hermes/skills`; agents and rules are reported as not installed. The
    plugin in `hermes/skilletor` runs skilletor's hooks when a Hermes session
    starts and on each turn, reports changes to the model, says how to opt in
    while `hermes` is not in `targets`, and fails open. On Windows it starts
    `bin/skilletor.exe`.
  - A source entry takes an optional `path`, a directory or a list of them
    relative to the source root, for repositories that keep skills outside
    `skills/<name>/`, such as `"path": ["skills", "optional-skills"]` for
    NousResearch/hermes-agent. Every directory beneath it that holds a
    `SKILL.md` is one skill, and the source then offers those skills only. A
    name found in two directories is an error of that skill alone. A config
    with `path` is a config error for skilletor 0.4.2 and earlier.
    Skills a sync installed there (carrying skilletor's `.gitignore`) are
    not offered, so a `path` into a `.claude/skills` offers the repo's own.
    Names that differ only in case count as one name on every system, as
    they share a directory on macOS and Windows.
  - `skilletor add` of a GitHub link into a directory (`.../tree/<ref>/<dir>`)
    sets `ref` and `path` instead of being refused.
  - Agent worktrees get the project's installed skills, agents and rules. A
    project that uses skilletor gets a fixed block in `.worktreeinclude`
    naming the paths skilletor installs to, its lock and
    `skilletor.local.json`; Claude Code copies the gitignored files it matches
    into every worktree it creates (`--worktree`, subagents with `isolation:
    worktree`, desktop parallel sessions). Commit the file once, when the
    report asks; your own lines in it stay untouched. A project synced before
    gets the block in its next session.
  - Windows: the background sync a prompt starts runs with its window
    hidden, so no console window can flash up.
  - A source entry needs no `git`, `url` or `local` of its own when another
    config file gives that source one: `{ "ref": "v2" }` or `{ "path": [...] }`
    in `skilletor.local.json` changes only that field of the project's source.
    A source no file gives a backend is a config error naming the source and
    each file declaring it. A user config entry without one changes projects
    only; a user install naming it is a config error.
  - `skilletor add` of a forge link: a `?query` or `#anchor` is dropped, also
    from a repository link; `/releases/tag/<tag>` and `/commit/<sha>` pin that
    ref; `%`-escapes in a link's ref and path are decoded (`%2F` puts a `/`
    into a ref); `Getty/karr/tree/v1` and `github:Getty/karr/tree/v1` pin the
    ref like `github.com/...` instead of dropping it. GitLab (`/-/tree/`,
    `/-/commit/`, `/-/tags/`), Codeberg (`/src/branch|tag|commit/`,
    `/releases/tag/`, `/commit/`) and Hugging Face (`/tree/`, `/commit/`,
    `datasets/` and `spaces/` repositories) links are read the same way, and
    a GitLab project path keeps every segment before `/-/` (nested groups).
    Any other page (`/issues`, `/pulls`, `/releases`, a segment past the
    repository such as `Getty/karr/extra`) is an error naming the repository
    to add, instead of a source that tracked HEAD or failed at fetch.
  - Names that differ only in case count as one name everywhere, not only
    under a source's `path`: `skills/Foo` beside `skills/foo` (or
    `agents/Bar.md` beside `agents/bar.md.njk`) in a source, in `.claude/`
    and in `plugin.json` skills are the duplicate-name error; `Foo@a` and
    `foo@b` in one install list are a duplicate target; two sources' wildcards
    offering `Foo` and `foo` skip both with a warning, and an explicit entry
    wins over another source's other spelling.
  - macOS, Windows: an item whose name changes only in case (`Foo@a` to
    `foo@a`, a source renaming `skills/Foo`), or a file inside an item
    renamed so, is no longer a conflict with its own old copy nor deleted
    with the old spelling after the sync wrote it: the file both spellings
    name is taken over (reported as added and removed, as on Linux).
    `uninstall foo@a` removes an entry `Foo@a`, and names the wildcard or
    bundle that installed `Foo`.
  - `"ref": null` in the user config or `skilletor.local.json` unpins a
    source: the `ref` a lower config file gave is dropped and `git` tracks
    the remote's HEAD again.
  - `skilletor source remove` refuses to remove the entry giving a source its
    only `git`, `url` or `local` while another config file keeps an entry for
    it without one, which would leave a config that no longer loads; the error
    names those files and how to remove them.

0.4.2     2026-10-06 05:28:16Z
  - Windows: hooks and the CLI start without Git Bash. `bin/skilletor.exe`, a
    small launcher next to the sh script, runs the bundle with the first
    `node.exe` on `PATH` and is the `skilletor` command in PowerShell and cmd.
  - Windows: a project synced there and on Linux gets the same files and the
    same lock. Sources are checked out with the repository's line endings,
    whatever `core.autocrlf` says, and paths inside an item, a lock key or a
    `.gitignore` line use `/`.
  - The Claude Code hooks are declared in exec form and start `bin/skilletor`
    without a shell.
  - A source name outside `^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$` is a config error.
  - Specs: an empty spec, a blank `github:` or forge owner, a shorthand owner,
    repo or port no forge takes, and an unknown `word:` prefix are errors. A
    scheme and `github:` match in any case, `.TAR.GZ` is a url, an empty repo
    segment in `github:` means the default repo, and git's
    `transport::address` form is a git source kept as written. A GitHub tree
    link is the repo at that ref; a file or subdirectory link is an error.
  - `add` refuses a non-https url source before writing anything, and refuses
    to keep an entry the config loader rejects, before trusting it.
  - `source remove`: a name the target config lacks is an error that names
    where else it is declared; an entry the config loader rejects can be
    removed when nothing else is refused.
  - Bundles: an entry address the config loader rejects is a bundle error, not
    a missing source, and a bundle's missing source never replaces one in the
    target config.
  - Catalog: `x.md` beside `x.md.njk` is an error of that agent or rule, and
    `f` beside `f.njk` inside a skill is an error of that skill.
  - `add`, `install`, `uninstall` and `source remove` exit 2 when their sync
    hits a config error.
  - A skill's scripts keep their executable bit. After the upgrade, one sync
    gives earlier installs and url caches theirs.

0.4.1     2026-09-26 22:58:47Z
  - First published release. The versions before it, 0.1.0 to 0.4.0, were
    development builds listed in the marketplace.
